 \section{The Biomimetic Humanoid Robot Project}\label{sec:project}
 
 This dissertation is part of a long-running effort at Portland State University to build a biomimetic humanoid robot from the trunk down, actuated by pneumatic artificial muscles and controlled by a synthetic nervous system (SNS). The project is deliberately iterative. Knowledge from biomechanics and neuroscience guides the design of robots intended to perform complex tasks in unstructured environments with improved efficiency, balance, adaptability, and resilience. Those robots then become physical models with which biological hypotheses can be tested, validated, and refined through controlled changes that would be impractical or unethical in living subjects. The resulting biological insight informs the next generation of robot design, and lessons from both directions can be translated into improved prosthetic and orthotic devices.
 
-The present work builds directly on a series of studies by the author and collaborators. In the author's master's project, the kinematics and dynamics of a biomimetic robot leg were matched against the Gait2392 OpenSim model, artificial muscle attachment and wrapping locations were determined for the robot legs, and MATLAB tooling was developed for evaluating muscle lengths, moment arms, and isometric joint torques over the full range of motion \citep{bolen_bipedal_2019, bolen_determination_2019}. That work showed that simply transplanting human muscle attachment locations onto a BPA-actuated robot does not reproduce human torque profiles; the placement optimization that closes this gap was completed during this dissertation and is summarized in Section~\ref{sec:foundation}. In parallel, a biomimetic four-bar knee linkage with a migrating instantaneous center of rotation was designed and validated by Steele \citep{steele_development_2017, steele_biomimetic_2018, steele_biomimetic_2018-1}, and a canine-inspired quadruped platform, Muscle Mutt, formerly called DoggyDeux, was developed with antagonistic BPA pairs and synthetic nervous system control \citep{scharzenberger_design_2019}. That platform extended an earlier AARL demonstration of central pattern generator control of a pneumatically actuated robot \citep{hunt_development_2017}.
